ta","data":[{"unit":1,"title":3,"area":95,"prev":97,"next":100,"titles":103,"firmware":23},{"id":2,"title":3,"aliases":4,"area":9,"order":10,"path":11,"context":12,"summary":17,"status":18,"firmware":19,"facts":24,"procedures":53,"parameters":54,"related":80,"body":83,"sources":84,"official_url":28,"controls":90},"fx.phaser","Phaser effect",[5,6,7,8],"phaser","phase shifter","phasing","sweep","fx",50,"knowledge/manual/units/fx/phaser.md",{"modes":13,"screens":15},[14],"auxiliary",[16],"M1","A 12-pole phaser for FX I or FX II; `M1` sets the centre frequency of the sweep, its depth, its rate and the feedback.","current",{"min":20,"changed_in":21,"guide_version":22,"verified_on":23},"1.0.9",[],"1.1.15","1.1.33",[25,31,34,37,42,47,50],{"id":26,"text":27,"source":28,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":30},"what","The phaser blends filtered copies of the sound with the dry signal; where the two are out of phase they cancel, which carves moving notches into the spectrum.","https://teenage.engineering/guides/op-xy/fx#phaser",null,"official",{"id":32,"text":33,"source":28,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":30},"poles","The phaser is a 12-pole design with 12 notches.",{"id":35,"text":36,"source":28,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":30},"feedback","More feedback (`E4`) sends more of the phaser's output back into it, making the sweep ring and sing.",{"id":38,"text":39,"source":40,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":23,"confidence":41},"labels","The phaser's columns read frequency, depth, rate and feedback, as the guide names them.","docs/research/59-screen-profiling.md#213-auxiliary-tracks","verified",{"id":43,"text":44,"source":45,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":46},"rate-range","The rate runs on one exponential curve from about 0.005 Hz at 0 to about 15 Hz at 99; one sweep takes about 2 s at rate 58, 1.4 s at 62, half a second at 75 and a fifth of a second at 84, and above about 90 the sweep is a wash rather than a movement (one video, OS 1.0, read from the ringing at high feedback).","docs/research/78-overnight-accuracy.md#log","community",{"id":48,"text":49,"source":45,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":46},"feedback-range","Feedback reaches about 0.9 at 99, enough for the notches to ring and sing; lower settings give a gentle effect.",{"id":51,"text":52,"source":45,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":46},"start-values","A phaser page opens on frequency 30, depth 61, rate 30 and feedback 50; frequency is the least certain of the four (one video, OS 1.0).",[],[55,63,69,75],{"screen":16,"encoder":56,"layer":57,"keys":58,"name":59,"range":29,"default":29,"cc":60,"cc_confidence":61,"note":62,"source":28,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":30},"E1","base","turn E1","frequency",12,"community-verified","centre frequency the sweep moves around",{"screen":16,"encoder":64,"layer":57,"keys":65,"name":66,"range":29,"default":29,"cc":67,"cc_confidence":61,"note":68,"source":28,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":30},"E2","turn E2","depth",13,"how far the sweep travels",{"screen":16,"encoder":70,"layer":57,"keys":71,"name":72,"range":29,"default":29,"cc":73,"cc_confidence":61,"note":74,"source":28,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":30},"E3","turn E3","rate",14,"sweep speed",{"screen":16,"encoder":76,"layer":57,"keys":77,"name":35,"range":29,"default":29,"cc":78,"cc_confidence":61,"note":79,"source":28,"firmware_min":29,"verified_on":29,"confidence":30},"E4","turn E4",15,"resonance of the notches",[81,82],"fx.overview","fx.chorus","A phaser gives a sound a slow, swirling motion. Frequency places the sweep, depth sets how far it\ntravels and rate how fast. With feedback low the effect is gentle; turned up it becomes a pronounced,\nwhistling resonance. It suits pads, chords and hi-hats.",[85,87,89],{"url":28,"kind":86},"guide",{"url":40,"kind":88},"research",{"url":45,"kind":88},[91,92,93,94],"encoder.1","encoder.2","encoder.3","encoder.4",